  "query_preview": "..\nWednesday through Thursday: A deep closed low dropping out of \nsouthern British Columbia will bring cooler temperatures to the \nregion, along with increasing showers on Wednesday. This is due \nto increased lift, as well as unstable conditions as 500mb \ntemperatures plunge -30C over the northern half of Washington into\nthe North Idaho Panhandle. This combined with lower level upslope\nflow from west- southwest winds at 850mb into the Cascade crest, \nand mountains of Northeast Washington and the North ID Panhandle \nwill produce several inches of snow. Bands of convergence into the\nCascades also has the potential to produce brief periods of \nheavier snow with HREF showing around a 30-40 percent chance of 1 \ninch per hour snow rates. A Winter Weather Advisory remains in \nplace for elevations above 3000 feet in the Cascades through 5 PM\nWednesday with the highest amounts expected over Stevens and \nWashington Passes. Wednesday Night into Thursday as the closed low\ndrops south to off the WA coast the flow turns southwest. Drier\nair coming into the region in the mid levels will result in a \ndecrease in the intensity and coverage of showers, but enough \nmoisture lingers over the northern mountains and Cascades for \nadditional light rain and mountain snow amounts. Below are the NBM\nchances for various snow thresholds being reached for the \nmountain passes from 5 PM Tuesday to 5 PM Thursday:\n\nMountain Pass   2+ inches   4+ inches   6+ inches   8+ inches\nStevens Pass    100%        95%         70%         50%\nWashington Pass 100%        75%         30%          5%     \nSherman Pass     50%        10%          0%          0% \nLookout Pass     90%        50%         10%          5% \n\nFriday through Tuesday: The influence of the upper low continues Friday \nand Saturday as another wave associated with the low passes\nthrough for more cool and showery conditions with mountain snow\nshowers, and a rain/snow mix in the valleys. On Sunday the low \nstarts to push out and is replaced by an upper ridge on Monday and Tuesday\nbringing dry weather and slightly warmer temperatures.",
